name  = startup
shape = the smallest useful program: read one argument, answer, exit
args  = 7
claim = the strongest claim Mere has against a managed runtime, and the one no
        other workload here measures. Every other row in this suite does enough
        work to bury process startup; this one does almost none, so what it
        reports is the cost of BEING a program in each language -- runtime
        initialisation, a garbage collector's first heap, an interpreter
        parsing its own standard library. Mere has none of those, and this is
        the row where that is worth something. It is also the shape a CLI tool
        and a per-request process actually have.
        The tiny amount of work is deliberate but not zero: each
        implementation parses the argument and prints a value derived from it,
        so a runtime cannot be credited for a program that was optimised away
        and the measurement stays a measurement of a program that ran.

# 104 B at v0.1.320 -- the whole program's allocation. The tightest band in the
# suite, and worth being tight: this row's claim is that Mere starts up like a C
# program rather than like a runtime, and the thing that would quietly break it
# is initialisation work creeping in. A ceiling of a few KB catches that on the
# first commit that adds it, where wall clock at this scale is mostly the
# scheduler.
alloc_min = 64
alloc_max = 4096

# MoonBit's row needs strconv as well as env.
mbt_imports = moonbitlang/core/env moonbitlang/core/strconv
